    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "The published **patch-clamp result should drive the initial safety decision**, because it measures the functional endpoint that matters: inhibition of hERG potassium current and consequent QT/TdP risk. Cisapride is a well-established hERG blocker, so a weak binding result should not override credible functional evidence.\n\nThe discrepancy can arise because radioligand displacement may not capture:\n\n- State- and use-dependent channel binding\n- Open/inactivated-channel preference\n- Voltage, temperature, and kinetics\n- Membrane partitioning or assay conditions\n- Differences in free concentrations and nonspecific binding\n\nTreat the binding result as a mechanistic or assay-validation datapoint, not evidence that risk is absent. Confirm the discrepancy with a qualified patch-clamp assay under relevant conditions, then interpret potency against clinically relevant unbound exposure, including metabolites and safety margins. Until reconciled, use the potent functional block as the conservative basis for decision-making.",
